m3uchoder - version 1
--
This program takes a m3u playlist and inserts breaks using a special proprietary algorithm™ (highly secret)

It works as follows:
After 15 minutes, or when there are less than 15 minutes remaining in the album and 10 minutes have passed since the last break, insert a regular break (referred internally as CHODE).
Upon reaching the last file in an album, if the remaining time between the end of the album and the last break is less than 10 minutes, insert a special break (referred internaly as UCHODE) otherwise, insert a regular break.

Albums changes are detected by checking if the name of the last folder in a file's path is different from the prior folder name, unless both the current and prior folders start with "D_I_".
Note that the current method of determining this will crash if the file path does not contain at least two folders (relative to the playlist)

Breaks
--
Breaks are defined at the top of m3uchoder.c
Using the default options:
regular breaks should be numbered in the format "Chode FM\CHODE FM <number>.mp3"
special breaks are defined in an array, they should be in the same folder as regular breaks.

remember to change the corresponding LEN_ variable when changing any of the strings, these should just be the number of characters in the string (excluding the null terminator)

Compiling
--
On unix, you will need FFmpeg's libavformat and libavutil as well as their associated headers, to compile simply run:
cc -lavformat -lavcodec m3uchoder.c -o m3uchoder

A Visual Studio project is provided for compiling on Windows, also provided are getline.c getdelim.c and strndup.c from NetBSD's libnbcompat, as Windows does not provide these functions.
You will need a build of ffmpeg with the development headers and library files, they should be put in the ffmpeg/ directory, the required files are detailed in ffmpeg/whatshouldbehere.txt

